import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
import JourneyNav from '@/components/ia/JourneyNav.astro';
---

<Layout title="Bounded contexts and common boundary" description="How SPDTF 2.0 working groups retain local meaning and agree a small interoperability boundary.">
  <PageMeta category="Ontology journey" variant="brand" updated="SPDTF 2.0 development · context ownership" />
  <h1>Bounded contexts and the common boundary</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A bounded context is the boundary within which a particular model and language are
    meaningful. SPDTF 2.0 starts with context-owned evidence, then makes the agreements
    between contexts explicit instead of forcing one universal definition too early.
  </p>
  <JourneyNav />

  <h2 id="contexts">Context ownership</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Initial working-group contexts</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Context</th><th scope="col">Illustrative scope</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Finance and Banking</th><td>Mortgage journey, lending decisions, parties, evidence, risk and finance-data exchange.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Conveyancing</th><td>Instruction, legal transfer, enquiries, searches, exchange and completion.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Estate Agency</th><td>Marketing, listings, material information, viewings, offers and seller information.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Surveying and Valuation</th><td>Inspection, condition, valuation, professional evidence and property risk.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Property Data Services</th><td>Registries, searches, authoritative sources, provenance, currency and reuse.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Property Technology</th><td>Platforms, workflow, integration, APIs, implementation and operational feedback.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">DBT Smart Data</th><td>OPDA-internal scheme context for participants, roles, trust, consent, authorisation, accreditation, liability and cross-sector alignment.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The DBT Smart Data group is not asserted to be another property bounded context and
    is not a government-established group. It supplies scheme-level questions without
    redefining meanings owned by the property groups.
  </p>

  <h2 id="interoperability">The small common boundary</h2>
  <p>
    The Interoperability Working Group is a peer of the domain groups. It owns only what
    is genuinely needed for exchange across boundaries:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>a deliberately small common boundary ontology;</li>
    <li>a context map showing where meanings originate and which group owns a decision;</li>
    <li>qualified mappings between related local terms, vocabularies and taxonomies;</li>
    <li>shared identifier, provenance, versioning and change conventions where needed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A concept belongs in the common boundary because multiple contexts need a stable
    exchange agreement, not because centralisation is convenient. A boundary agreement is
    not a redesign of a domain model.
  </p>

  <h2 id="evidence">Evidence becomes a candidate</h2>
  <p>
    Participants bring language, rules, examples, documents and scenarios. Facilitators
    separate context-owned evidence from cross-context questions. Candidates keep the
    source, open questions, review evidence and disposition that explain how a meaning
    was proposed.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/spdtf-2/ontologies/coverage">Next: coverage crosswalk →</a></p>
</Layout>
